{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>My statutes you shall keep.</b> And what are they? 'Your beast you shall not mate as a mixed breed; your field you shall not sow with mixed seed; and a garment of mixed stuff (sha'atnez) shall not come upon you.'" "<b>By way of the plain sense (peshat).</b> The reason for the prohibition of mixed species (kil'ayim) is because all created things on earth, whether animal or plant, have a force and a mazal above, and each and every one was created according to its species — for so the King, may He be exalted, established in the work of Creation, that each and every one should be 'after its kind' (למינהו). And whoever mates or grafts two species is changing and contradicting the work of Creation, where it is written למינהו, and he does the opposite of HaShem's will — for He desired to separate between the species — as though he shows himself to think that the created beings that the Holy One, blessed be He, created in His world do not suffice, and he wishes to be clever and to add there newly devised species beyond all that the Creator of the world made. And the prohibition of plowing with an ox and a donkey together also follows this same reason, for it is the way of farmers after their plowing to bring the animals into one stall, and they come to crossbreeding, to produce strange foreign species differing in their offspring, contradicting the work of Creation." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'Your field you shall not sow with mixed seed' — this is what the verse says (Isaiah 42:21): 'HaShem desired, for the sake of His righteousness, to magnify the Torah and make it glorious.' The Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Torah to Israel in order to bequeath them the life of the World to Come, and He left no thing without giving a commandment in it to Israel: if one comes to plow — 'you shall not plow with an ox and a donkey together'; if one comes to sow — 'your field you shall not sow with mixed seed'; if one comes to reap — 'you shall not glean the harvest' (לקט קצירך לא תלקט); if one comes to dress — 'you shall not dress with sha'atnez,' 'and a garment of mixed stuff (sha'atnez) shall not come upon you.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> In the secret of the commandment of mixed species (kil'ayim): this commandment is among the commandments called 'statutes' (חקים), and the ultimate intent of these statutes is not in physical matters alone, but in the intellectual forces that flow from heaven. Therefore they are called 'the statutes of heaven,' as it is written (Job 38:33): 'Do you know the statutes of heaven (חקות שמים)?' And these statutes were given only to Israel, as it is written (Psalms 147:19): 'His statutes and judgments to Israel,' and it is written (Psalms 81:5): 'For it is a statute (חק) for Israel.' And all the supernal intellectual forces, each and every one, is appointed over its charge and its governance, and the actions in this lowly world draw down in their causal sequence, and through this the world persists and stands upon its perfection. And you already know their saying, of blessed memory: 'Even the beings above need peace,' as it is said (Job 25:2): 'He makes peace in His heights' — and their peace is their persistence and their performing their function in their appointed roles. If so, one who breeds offspring below, species with its own species, brings about peace above, for the appointed forces are harmonized in their roles as the lowly world requires. But one who breeds offspring below, species with a species not its own — this brings about the opposite of peace, for he mixes the supernal forces and nullifies them and uproots them from their appointments. And this is the secret of the word כלאים, which is a term of prevention (מניעה), meaning nullification of the forces, from the language of (Psalms 40:12): 'Do not withhold (תכלא) Your mercies from me,' and likewise (Jeremiah 37:18): 'the house of confinement (בית הכלא),' for one placed there is prevented from his work. And this is the meaning of 'statutes' (חקים) — from the language of engraving and depiction (חקיקה וציור), for they are the depictions of supernal intellectual matters, and one who mates an animal of one species with one not of its species, or one who sows a plant of one species with one not of its species, and they draw nurture one from the other — this mixes the supernal forces that grant the power of generation or the power of growth. As the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'There is no herb below that does not have a mazal above that strikes it and says to it, Grow!' as it is said (Job 38:33): 'Do you know the statutes of heaven (חקות שמים),' and so forth. And because the two firstborn beings in the world were kil'ayim — this one from the side of the good spirit and that one from the side of the evil spirit — and we were commanded to follow the Holy Spirit and to distance ourselves from evil, which is from the side of the spirit of impurity, therefore mixed species were forbidden to us, for it is a joining of two extremes that are opposites; it is not fitting to join them, for their joining does not turn out well, but it is fitting to distance them and to separate them one from the other. And one who separates between them brings peace to the world, and one who joins them together removes peace from the world, for he mixes the supernal forces and sets accusation among the retinue on high. And kil'ayim were forbidden to us in three things: in animals, in seeds, and in garments. What was forbidden to us in animals — because the nature of procreation is the same in man and beast. What was forbidden to us in the seed of the field — because woman is likened to a field, being prepared to receive offspring like a field. What was forbidden to us in garments — that is, wool and linen — because the firstborn beings' offering was wool and linen: this one brought wool and this one brought linen; therefore joining wool and linen together was forbidden to us, because the joining of both in one womb did not turn out well, for there was in it a mixture of forces causing what is the opposite of peace, and its end proved its beginning, for this one murdered that one in the end, and both perished from the world. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: sha'atnez — 'shua' (combed), 'tavuy' (spun), and 'nuz' (woven) — for this one alone and that one alone is permitted. And therefore linen is called in the holy tongue 'bad' (בד), meaning singular (יחידי), for it is forbidden to join it with wool; and likewise wool with linen, for the joining is what is forbidden. And one who wears such a garment below mixes the supernal forces. And Israel were warned against this joining, since they are opposites, in order to distance oneself from the spirit of impurity and to be sanctified with the Holy Spirit. And it says: 'It shall not come upon you (לא יעלה עליך)' — that is, the spirit of impurity will come upon him, for by wearing a garment of kil'ayim, sha'atnez, the spirit of impurity ascends upon him, for it is a joining of two opposites whose joining does not turn out well. And that which kil'ayim were permitted in tzitzit, and also in the garments of the priests — but only at the time of service — is because all the forces that differ one from another are all included in the commandment of tzitzit, between the white and the blue (תכלת). And this is the secret of the verse that says (Proverbs 31:13): 'A woman of valor' — and this is the attribute of the blue (תכלת) — 'she seeks (דרשה) wool and linen, and works with willing hands' — it says that that attribute seeks and investigates the transgressor and joins wool and linen together in order to punish him; and on the other hand, 'and works with willing hands' — in tzitzit. And they are all included in the commandment of tzitzit. So too in the Holy Temple at the time of service, no explanation is needed, for all the forces are included there, for all matters of the Temple below are all patterned after the matters of the Temple on high."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:19.597523559Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}